diagnosis_text:
The interdomain routing “handshake” (eBGP) between the border routers is currently healthy and exchanging routes correctly. From router1 (AS1) and router2 (AS2), BGP is established over the interconnect 193.10.11.0/24 on eth0. Router1’s RIB contains a BGP-learned route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2, and router2’s RIB contains a BGP-learned route to 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. End-to-end probes confirm pc1 (195.11.14.2) can reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTT. This means forwarding and control-plane “handshake” are both working.

Mechanism: If the eBGP session were down, router1 would lack a route to 200.1.1.0/24 and pc1→pc2 traffic would fail. Instead, “show ip bgp summary” on both routers shows the neighbor up for over a minute, with prefixes received/sent as expected, which aligns with successful pings.

Localization: FRR BGP on router1 and router2, interface eth0 on each, and kernel FIB entries for the partner LANs.

Root cause: No active fault found; the earlier outage cannot be reproduced—likely a transient BGP flap or external condition that has since cleared.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the issue recurs, enable BFD or tighten BGP keepalive/hold timers for faster detection, and collect FRR logs around the event to identify any brief session drops; otherwise, the network path to 200.1.1.2 is operational now.
